Maintainer notes for the carlos-emr Debian packaging
=====================================================

Pre-release version ladder (READ BEFORE CUTTING ANY PRE-RELEASE)
----------------------------------------------------------------

dpkg orders `~` pre-release suffixes by plain ASCII comparison, so the
conventional identifiers sort:

    ~alphaN  <  ~betaN  <  ~rcN  <  ~snapshotN  <  (final, no suffix)

`docs/release-process.md` allows a train's Maven snapshot to advance through
alpha, beta, or release-candidate identifiers. The deb version MUST NOT
mechanically follow that ladder once a `~snapshotN` deb has shipped: because
`s` sorts after `a`, `b` and `r`, a later `2026.09.0~beta1` or `2026.09.0~rc1`
package would compare LOWER than the `~snapshot2` already installed on tester
machines — apt would treat the newer build as a downgrade and hold it back
(or unattended-upgrades would silently never apply it).

The rule for this packaging:

  * Once a `~snapshotN` deb has shipped on a release train, every later
    pre-release deb on that train is `~snapshotN+1`, and the release itself
    is the bare final version (`2026.09.0`), which sorts above every `~`
    suffix. Never emit `~alpha`, `~beta` or `~rc` after a `~snapshot`.

  * If a future train wants a labeled alpha/beta/rc ladder in the deb
    version, use it from the train's FIRST deb and never mix `~snapshot`
    into it (alpha < beta < rc < final already sort correctly on their own).

Check any two candidate versions before publishing:

    dpkg --compare-versions '2026.09.0~snapshot2' lt '2026.09.0~rc1' \
        && echo ok || echo "DOWNGRADE - do not ship"

Other conventions
-----------------

  * Source format is `3.0 (native)`: this repository packages itself; there
    is no upstream tarball.
  * debhelper compat is pinned at 13 on purpose — at 14, dh_installtmpfiles
    would emit a `systemd-tmpfiles --purge` postrm snippet that could remove
    the patient document store. See the comment in debian/control before
    bumping it.
  * Maintainer scripts follow the rule documented at the top of
    debian/carlos-emr.postrm: no lifecycle transition ever destroys clinical
    data or the material needed to read it.
